 ### [1D Transfer Function Editor](8746-1d-transfer-function-editor.md)
-A user interface component that maps scalar voxel values to optical properties using a 1D curve editor with control points. It solves the problem of interactive classification by allowing users to visually define visibility. It is distinguished from [Transfer Function Mapping via 1D Textures](8608-transfer-function-mapping-via-1d-textures.md) by its explicit UI-based control point manipulation, and from [Piecewise Constant Transfer Function](12134-piecewise-constant-transfer-function.md) by its support for continuous interpolation between knots.
+A user interface component that maps scalar voxel values to optical properties using a 1D curve editor with control points. It solves the problem of interactive classification by allowing users to visually define visibility. It is distinguished from [Transfer Function Mapping via 1D Textures](8608-transfer-function-mapping-via-1d-textures.md) by its explicit UI-based control point manipulation, and from [Piecewise Constant Transfer Function](12134-piecewise-constant-transfer-function.md) by its support for continuous interpolation between knots. Choose this when interactive, real-time tuning of opacity and color curves is required.
 
 ### [Piecewise Constant Transfer Function](12134-piecewise-constant-transfer-function.md)
-Maps voxel intensity values to RGBA using disjoint, non-overlapping intervals, creating a "step" response [12134-piecewise-constant-transfer-function.md]. It is distinguished from the parent [Transfer Function](1272-transfer-function.md) by its lack of interpolation (binary classification) [12134-piecewise-constant-transfer-function.md]. It differs from [Transfer Function Mapping via 1D Textures](8608-transfer-function-mapping-via-1d-textures.md) by not requiring texture sampling or linear interpolation [12134-piecewise-constant-transfer-function.md].
+Maps voxel intensity values to RGBA using disjoint, non-overlapping intervals, creating a "step" response. It is distinguished from the parent [Transfer Function](1272-transfer-function.md) by its lack of interpolation (binary classification). It differs from [Transfer Function Mapping via 1D Textures](8608-transfer-function-mapping-via-1d-textures.md) by not requiring texture sampling or linear interpolation. Choose this when strict threshold-based segmentation is needed without blending between material classes. [G]
 
 ### 3D Pre-integrated Transfer Function
-A 3D texture-based TF used during optical property accumulation to handle varying sampling frequencies and constant brick values [S19, unofficial]. It stores the function in a 3D texture accessed via floating-point coordinates [S19, unofficial]. It allows maintaining constant brightness and provides better rendering quality with adaptive sampling compared to standard TFs [S19, unofficial].
+A 3D texture-based TF used during optical property accumulation to handle varying sampling frequencies and constant brick values. It stores the function in a 3D texture accessed via floating-point coordinates. It allows maintaining constant brightness and provides better rendering quality with adaptive sampling compared to standard TFs. Choose this when high-quality pre-integrated compositing is required for complex brick-based rendering pipelines. [S19, unofficial]
 
 ### Local Singular Transfer Function
-A local rendering approach using a lookup table to assign texture values (color/opacity) to interpolated points (IDs) prior to global composition [S20, unofficial]. It addresses the insufficiency of global multi-dimensional TFs for complex datasets [S20, unofficial]. It uses a texture assignment table where each object ID has a one-to-one relationship with specific RGBA values [S20, unofficial].
+A local rendering approach using a lookup table to assign texture values (color/opacity) to interpolated points (IDs) prior to global composition. It addresses the insufficiency of global multi-dimensional TFs for complex datasets. It uses a texture assignment table where each object ID has a one-to-one relationship with specific RGBA values. Choose this when segment-based or ID-driven local classification outperforms global scalar mapping. [S20, unofficial]
 
